      "prompt": {
        "question": "What is Euler's formula?",
        "hint": "$e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta)$"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) - i\\sin(\\theta)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$e^{i\\theta} = \\sin(\\theta) + i\\cos(\\theta)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Correct! Euler's formula: $e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta)$. One of the most beautiful equations in mathematics!",
          "insight": "Euler's formula connects exponential, trigonometric, and complex functions. Special case: $e^{i\\pi} + 1 = 0$ (Euler's identity)."
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "Close, but that's the formula for $e^{-i\\theta}$ (complex conjugate). Euler's formula has a plus sign.",
          "2": "The order matters! Cosine comes first: $\\cos + i\\sin$, not $\\sin + i\\cos$."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Recalled Euler's formula"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-2",
      "order": 2,
      "currentState": {
        "description": "Apply Euler's formula with $\\theta = \\pi/4$."
      },
      "prompt": {
        "question": "What are $\\cos(\\pi/4)$ and $\\sin(\\pi/4)$?",
        "hint": "Both equal $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ (or $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$). This is 45° angle."
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$\\cos(\\pi/4) = \\sin(\\pi/4) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$\\cos(\\pi/4) = \\frac{1}{2}$, $\\sin(\\pi/4) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$\\cos(\\pi/4) = \\sin(\\pi/4) = \\frac{1}{2}$"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Perfect! At $\\pi/4$ radians (45°), we have an isosceles right triangle. Both sine and cosine equal $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 0.707$.",
          "insight": "Key angles to memorize: 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90° (in radians: 0, π/6, π/4, π/3, π/2). At 45°, sine and cosine are equal."
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "Those are values for $\\pi/3$ (60°). At $\\pi/4$ (45°), both sine and cosine equal $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.",
          "2": "Close to the decimal value, but exact value is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, not $\\frac{1}{2}$."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Found cos and sin values"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step-3",
      "order": 3,
      "currentState": {
        "description": "Write in rectangular form."
      },
      "prompt": {
        "question": "What is $e^{i\\pi/4}$ in form $a + bi$?",
        "hint": "$e^{i\\pi/4} = \\cos(\\pi/4) + i\\sin(\\pi/4) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$e^{i\\pi/4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i$ or $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(1 + i)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$e^{i\\pi/4} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}i$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$e^{i\\pi/4} = 1 + i$"
        }
      ],
      "correctOptionIndex": 0,
      "feedback": {
        "correct": {
          "explanation": "Excellent! $e^{i\\pi/4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(1 + i) \\approx 0.707 + 0.707i$. This complex number has magnitude 1 (on unit circle) and argument $\\pi/4$ radians.",
          "insight": "All numbers $e^{i\\theta}$ lie on the unit circle. The angle $\\theta$ gives the direction, and Euler's formula provides the rectangular coordinates!"
        },
        "incorrect": {
          "1": "That's $e^{i\\pi/3}$ (60° angle), not $e^{i\\pi/4}$ (45° angle).",
          "2": "Close direction, but wrong magnitude! Should be $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(1+i)$, which has magnitude 1. Your answer $1+i$ has magnitude $\\sqrt{2}$."
        }
      },
      "resultState": {
        "description": "Expressed in rectangular form"
      }
    }
