  "summary": {
    "keyInsights": [
      "Euler's formula: $e^{i\\theta} = \\cos(\\theta) + i\\sin(\\theta)$",
      "At $\\theta = \\pi/4$ (45°): $\\cos(\\pi/4) = \\sin(\\pi/4) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$",
      "Result: $e^{i\\pi/4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(1+i)$",
      "This point lies on unit circle at 45° angle",
      "Magnitude: $|e^{i\\pi/4}| = 1$, Argument: $\\arg(e^{i\\pi/4}) = \\pi/4$"
    ],
    "fullSolution": "Apply Euler's formula: e^(iπ/4) = cos(π/4) + i·sin(π/4). Since π/4 radians = 45°, both cos(π/4) and sin(π/4) equal √2/2. Therefore: e^(iπ/4) = (√2/2) + (√2/2)i = (√2/2)(1 + i) ≈ 0.707 + 0.707i.",
